<title>BELGIUM: EU says could export some subsidy-free wheat.</title>
<headline>EU says could export some subsidy-free wheat.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS, ' 1997-04-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>European Commission director general for agriculture Guy Legras said on Wednesday that the European Union could export wheat without subsidy between 1997 and 2005 but not regularly.  </p>
<p>&quot;It's possible that world prices could be higher than EU prices and therefore we could export without subsidy,&quot; Legras told a news briefing.</p>
<p>&quot;It happened last year and could happen again but can't be considered as a normal situation,&quot; he added.</p>
<p>Legras said the EU had kept export refunds at a very low level in the season that had just effectively ended.</p>
<p>&quot;We exported a large quantity and kept prices relatively low for the past 10 months,&quot; he said, but added that the situation was too uncertain to speculate about next season's export policy due to doubts about frost damage in the U.S. and until the EU crop outlook was clearer.</p>
<p>-- Brussels Newsroom +32 2 287 6800</p>
</text>
